WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.510
Progress measurement keeps deliberate practice honest.

2
00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:07.950
Without measurement, practice can become a ritual that consumes time without improving capability.

3
00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:10.460
A baseline shows where performance starts.

4
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:20.921
Metrics, rubrics, examples of good work, decision records, practice logs, and before-and-after comparisons can all help show whether the learner is actually improving.

5
00:00:20.921 --> 00:00:24.268
Good measurement includes both qualitative and quantitative evidence.

6
00:00:24.268 --> 00:00:34.310
A quantitative measure might count time to complete a task, number of defects found, number of strong hypotheses generated, or score on a rubric.

7
00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:40.168
Qualitative evidence might include better reasoning, clearer explanations, stronger prioritization, or fewer repeated mistakes.

8
00:00:40.168 --> 00:00:43.933
The right measure depends on the skill being practiced.

9
00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:46.025
Calibration is part of progress.

10
00:00:46.025 --> 00:00:50.209
A learner should understand how confidence compares to actual performance.

11
00:00:50.209 --> 00:00:55.230
If confidence rises faster than capability, practice may be creating false certainty.

12
00:00:55.230 --> 00:01:02.343
If capability improves but confidence stays too low, the learner may not use the skill when needed.

13
00:01:02.343 --> 00:01:08.620
Decision records and review notes help learners compare what they expected with what actually happened.

14
00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:09.875
Avoid vanity metrics.

15
00:01:09.875 --> 00:01:18.243
Hours practiced, pages read, or tasks completed may be useful context, but they do not prove skill growth by themselves.

16
00:01:18.243 --> 00:01:21.590
The core question is whether real capability improved.

17
00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:33.306
Measurement should help decide whether to continue the current practice, adjust the target, seek better feedback, or move to the next subskill because the current bottleneck has improved.

18
00:01:33.306 --> 00:01:37.072
A good progress review should lead to a decision.

19
00:01:37.072 --> 00:01:41.256
Keep the same drill if the learner is still improving.

20
00:01:41.256 --> 00:01:46.695
Change the drill if the measure is no longer connected to real performance.

21
00:01:46.695 --> 00:01:50.461
Increase difficulty if the task has become too easy.

22
00:01:50.461 --> 00:01:56.737
Return to a simpler component if errors show that the foundation is not stable yet.
